1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,520
An electrode contacts the body; a lead is the electrical view
calculated from electrode signals. A standard twelve-lead ECG uses ten
electrodes to create twelve views. Limb leads are not excluded from
the diagnostic ECG merely because lead two is also commonly used for
rhythm monitoring.

2
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:39,048
Bipolar limb leads compare two poles. Augmented limb and precordial
leads use a positive exploring electrode and a calculated reference.
The same activation sequence can therefore appear upright in one lead
and negative in another without representing two different heartbeats.

3
00:00:39,048 --> 00:01:01,584
Continuous monitoring tracks rate and rhythm over time, commonly
displaying lead two or a modified chest lead. Diagnostic twelve-lead
acquisition records multiple anatomical views with suitable quality
and filter settings. The difference is purpose and acquisition
configuration, not a rule that certain standard leads are monitoring-
only.

4
00:01:01,584 --> 00:01:23,760
MCL1 is near a V1 monitoring viewpoint and MCL6 near V6, but their
configuration and reference differ from a standard diagnostic
recording. Follow the device instructions and local protocol. Never
relabel a modified monitoring view as a correctly acquired standard
twelve-lead ECG.

5
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:43,128
Explain the procedure and seek cooperation before exposing the chest.
Maintain draping and reveal only the area needed. For a patient with
breasts, use the correct chest-wall landmarks rather than placing
electrodes on breast tissue when feasible, while preserving dignity
and consent.

6
00:01:43,128 --> 00:02:03,240
A history of trauma, cultural concerns, pain or patient preference may
affect placement. Discuss a workable approach without coercion.
Document any declined or modified placement and its interpretive
limitation, rather than quietly treating a nonstandard recording as
standard.

7
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:23,160
Place limb electrodes on appropriate clean, flat limb areas, avoiding
poor contact over bone or joints. The school uses the AHA colour
convention: right arm white, left arm black, right leg green and left
leg red. Read the cable labels because other colour systems exist.

8
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:44,136
Limb reversal or unrecorded relocation can change axis and waveform
appearance. If a tracing differs unexpectedly, recheck connections and
anatomy before assuming a new disease. A genuine negative QRS may
still be physiological or pathological after placement has been
verified.

9
00:02:44,136 --> 00:03:04,968
Identify ribs from anatomical landmarks rather than estimating by eye.
Place V1 at the fourth intercostal space on the right sternal border
and V2 at the fourth space on the left. Locate V4 at the fifth
intercostal space on the midclavicular line before positioning V3
between V2 and V4.

10
00:03:04,968 --> 00:03:23,712
V5 lies on the anterior axillary line and V6 on the midaxillary line,
both horizontally level with V4. Do not follow the rib space downward
around the chest. Check all six positions before acquiring, and
document unavoidable departures from standard placement.

11
00:03:23,712 --> 00:03:43,608
When the net depolarization vector moves toward a lead’s positive
viewpoint, it tends to produce an upward deflection; movement away
tends downward. Lead two commonly aligns with normal ventricular
activation, which explains its usefulness, but it is not guaranteed to
be the largest or always upright.

12
00:03:43,608 --> 00:04:01,632
Unexpected polarity is a reason to inspect placement and connections,
not a command to rearrange electrodes until the tracing looks normal.
Once technical errors are corrected, preserve the real finding, obtain
appropriate additional views and integrate the patient’s condition.
